# User-Level CLAUDE.md

## Claude Rules

- Keep responses focused and brief. Lead with the outcome. Keep output short by leaving out details that do not change what I would do next, not by compressing prose into fragments. When explaining, give the high-level version unless I ask for depth.
- Do not use em dashes.
{{- if eq .machine "work" }}

## Formatting

Formatting and lint autofix run automatically on save via a PostToolUse hook, so don't run ruff format, make format, or make check just to tidy an edit. If the hook reports it reformatted a file, re-read it before editing again.
{{- end }}

## Code comments

Default to **zero** comments. Naming carries the meaning; a comment is an admission the code failed to.

Write a comment only when it explains a *why* the code cannot:

- A business rule or spec constraint that looks arbitrary otherwise.
- A workaround for an upstream bug or platform quirk (name it, link it).
- A non-obvious invariant or ordering dependency that will break if someone reorders.

Never:

- Restate the line below it (`# increment counter`, `# loop over rows`).
- Narrate the edit for the reviewer (`# changed to use X`, `# new`, `# added for TPID support`). That belongs in the PR body, not the source.
- Add banner or section dividers (`# ---- Helpers ----`).
- Add a `TODO` unless I asked for one.

Docstrings: one line, imperative, only when the name plus signature genuinely leave the purpose ambiguous. No `Args:` / `Returns:` / `Raises:` blocks unless a parameter has a non-obvious contract. Types are in the signature already, do not restate them.

Length cap: one line per comment. If it needs a paragraph, it is documentation, put it in the README or the PR.

## Reuse before you add

Look in the repo's shared modules and config first.

- Before adding a constant, config value, helper, macro, or fixture, search for an existing one and import it. If the new code can't reach it, move it somewhere both can.
- A test that keeps two copies in sync means there should be one copy: merge them. Sync checks are only for state outside the repo and for generated files.
- When two functions, macros, or models differ by one detail, make that detail a parameter.
- When the change works, reread the diff next to the code around it and fold in anything that repeats or nearly repeats existing code.
- Only merge duplication that exists now. Don't add an abstraction for a single caller.
- A small consolidation the change needs goes in the same PR. A refactor that touches unrelated callers goes in its own PR, merged first.

## Pull requests

Use the `create-pr` command to open PRs. Whatever writes the body, never include: a file-by-file walkthrough or table of changed files (git shows that), restated diff content or before/after snippets, or emoji headings, "Overview"/"Background" preamble, and closing summary paragraphs.

When I ask for a PR comment, output it as raw markdown in a code block so I can copy it verbatim. Same brevity rules apply.

## Python projects

For any Python work, existing repos or new projects alike, follow the `python-conventions` skill (uv, pyproject.toml, Makefile targets, Ruff, mypy, pytest, Click, loguru).
